Added refresh tokens, rotated on every use and revoked on replay. A refresh token is issued alongside the code grant when the client is **registered** for the `refresh_token` grant — gated on the registration rather than on a scope, so whether a client may keep working without its user present is an operator's decision recorded where every other client permission lives, not something a client grants itself by asking. The client-credentials grant never gets one (RFC 6749 §4.4.3): a client that can mint a token whenever it likes with its own secret gains nothing from a second credential. A refresh request may narrow its scope but never widen it, or consent would be a one-time formality.

Rotation on its own buys very little — an attacker holding a stolen copy simply uses it first, and the legitimate client is the one whose token stops working. What makes it worth doing is **reuse detection**. Every token descended from one authorization shares a family, spent tokens are remembered rather than merely deleted, and a spent token turning up again means two parties hold it while exactly one is entitled to it. Since there is no way to tell which, the whole family is revoked and both are sent back to the authorization endpoint, where a human is involved again. One compromised chain does not disturb any other. Every refusal answers with the same message, because a client that can tell "already used" from "never existed" learns whether a token it stole has been rotated since.

The lifetime is configurable as `pas.plugins.identity.server_refresh_token_ttl`, defaulting to fourteen days: long enough that a daily integration never sends a human to a login page, short enough that an abandoned one stops working inside a sprint. The store sweeps expired tokens and expired replay records on every write, so it cannot grow without bound in a site where most integrations are set up once and forgotten. `refresh_token` now appears in the discovery document's `grant_types_supported`, which is built from the same constant the token endpoint dispatches on — an advertised grant nothing implements is a lie a client acts on. @ericof
